# Register a conjecture

Background and rules: `docs/discipline/conjecture-ledger.md`. The ledger is
`wiki/conjectures/`; the format is `wiki/conjectures/TEMPLATE.md`.

## 1. Check it is new, and that it is open

- **Is this already known?** Ask it before anything else. If it is proved in the literature,
  it is not a conjecture.
{{#if modules.known_results}}
  Record it in `wiki/known-results/` instead, with its citation, and stop here.
{{else}}
  Do not register it; note the citation in the file that prompted the thought.
{{/if}}
  If you can prove it yourself by a short argument, it is the project's own result: register
  it all the same, write the proof as its evidence, and take it to `proved` by the
  `gate-chain` skill, so the `proof-referee` reads the proof{{#if modules.known_results}} (the known-results catalog
  holds only what others proved){{/if}}.
- Read `wiki/conjectures/_index.md`, including `refuted` rows: a refuted conjecture with
  the same statement and family means the answer is already known.
{{#if modules.known_results}}
- Check `wiki/known-results/`. If a published result only suggests it (a different family,
  a weaker form), cite that as its origin. One that settles it is the case above: it goes in
  known-results, not here.
{{/if}}
- If it is a sharper or wider version of an existing conjecture, add it to that file as a
  `@residue:` line instead, unless it needs its own status.
- **What is it known as?** If it came from a pattern (a closed form, a recurrence, an
  algebraic or combinatorial structure), list candidate names for that structure and search
  under each{{#if modules.firsthand}} (the literature search in `docs/discipline/firsthand-checks.md`){{/if}} before
  treating it as new. A known name brings a literature with it.

## 2. State it

Write the statement as a connection between two properties of the same objects, on a named
family: "for the objects in F, A holds exactly when B does" (or "only when", or "A
determines B"). Put the family in `family:` (a parameter range, a class of objects, a named
sequence) and keep the statement inside it.

Examples of the shape (illustrations from several fields; yours will be in {{project.field}}):
- Number theory: "For primes 5 <= l <= 31, p(l n + b) = 0 mod l for all n only when
  24 b = 1 mod l."
- Combinatorics: "For n <= 8, every cap set of maximum size in F_3^n is, up to affine maps,
  a product of maximum caps in lower dimensions."
- Geometry: "For point sets in general position with n <= 30, the maximum number of unit
  distances is attained only by subsets of a lattice."

A single-property statement is acceptable when the question asks for exactly that property
(an exact value, a bound). If you think another shape fits better, say so to {{owner}} in
plain mathematical terms and file it as {{owner}} decides (`docs/discipline/conjecture-ledger.md`).

Write its `## Reading`: the other plausible readings and conventions, then the intended one
and why. If you cannot tell which reading is meant, say so in your report.

**Check it against the examples bank.** Run the statement against the `## Examples` of
each question it will serve, breakers and outside probes first. If a bank example breaks it,
either restate it or record it as refuted with that example: it never enters the ledger
untested against what broke earlier claims. If you restate it, the first evidence entry of
the restated conjecture names the original statement and the example that broke it, with a
`repair:` line (monster-barring, exception or lemma-incorporation;
`docs/discipline/informal-arguments.md`).

## 3. Choose the question(s) and write the tie

Run `bin/question-check --tsv` and read `wiki/questions/_index.md`. Pick the question(s)
whose answer a verdict here would change, and write `question-tie:` as one sentence giving
the consequence: "if true, answer shape A2 holds for this family; if refuted, A1 is ruled
out". Being about the same objects is not a tie (see `docs/discipline/question-ties.md`).

If no question fits, do not stretch a tie: propose a question with the `new-question`
skill, and register the conjecture linking it (it stays parked until the owner activates
that question).

## 4. File it

1. Copy `wiki/conjectures/TEMPLATE.md` to `wiki/conjectures/CNNN-<slug>.md` with the next
   free number.
2. `status: open`. Fill `questions:`, `question-tie:`, `family:` and the `# CNNN:` heading.
3. Under `## Evidence`, note where the conjecture came from (a computation, a paper, a
   pattern in other entries), dated.
4. Under `## Next steps`, write at least one `@residue:` line: the cheapest way to break it.
   Where small cases say little about it, a proof attempt or a reduction may come first,
   with the cheap way to break it beside it (`docs/discipline/conjecture-ledger.md`).
5. Add a row to the table in `wiki/conjectures/_index.md`.
6. Run `bin/question-check` and fix every FAIL.
7. Write your proposed `credence:` and `pursuit:` into the file, each with its date and
   reason, ending `(proposed by an agent, <date>)`. {{owner}} confirms or changes them, which
   removes the mark; mention them in your report.
{{#if modules.attempts}}
8. Read `bin/attempts --obstructions <Q>` for each question it serves. If the conjecture,
   once settled, would remove a recorded obstruction, say so in that line's log
   (`bin/attempts log <id> "<CNNN> would remove this obstruction if it holds"`).
{{/if}}

Never write `@priority`; that is {{owner}}'s. If you think it deserves priority, say so in
your report, with the reason.

## 5. Report

{{#if harness.cap.ask_tool}}
Tell {{owner}} the id, the statement, the question it serves and the first residue. A
decision you need now (for example, a new question it depends on) can go through the
structured ask tool; otherwise put it in your report.
{{else}}
Tell {{owner}}, in your report, the id, the statement, the question it serves and the first
residue, and any new question it depends on.
{{/if}}
